    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An Andean republic formed in 1830 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-district-gran-colombia">Gran Colombia’s Southern District</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> and Cuenca, became independent.</li><li>Politics long pitted Catholic, conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> against liberal, commercial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-garcia-moreno">García Moreno</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eloy-alfaro">Eloy Alfaro</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">Cacao</a>, bananas and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> oil drove its economy; it lost most of its Amazon claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1942 and adopted the dollar in 2000.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Ecuador’s 2008 constitution was the first in the world to give nature itself enforceable rights, allowing any person to go to court on behalf of a river or forest.</p></aside>
    </section>

