    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre.jpg" alt="Antonio José de Sucre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_Jos%C3%A9_de_Sucre._Michelena,_Arturo._1895,_Legislative_Palace,_La_Paz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Arturo Michelena, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic of Ecuador grew out of the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/real-audiencia-of-quito">Audiencia of Quito</a>, the Spanish colonial jurisdiction covering the northern Andes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> elite formed one of the earliest Spanish American juntas on 10 August 1809, but royalist forces suppressed it, and independence came only with the campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a>, whose victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pichincha">Battle of Pichincha</a> on 24 May 1822 freed Quito. The territory was then incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> Colombia as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-district-gran-colombia">Southern District</a>. Resentment of distant government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, heavy war taxes and the union’s own disintegration led an assembly of notables in Quito to declare the district independent on 13 May 1830, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> and Cuenca followed within days. A constituent congress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riobamba">Riobamba</a> adopted a first constitution that September and named the new state after the equator, a neutral name that avoided the regional pride attached to Quito. The Venezuelan-born general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-flores">Juan José Flores</a>, who had married into the Quito aristocracy, became its first president. In 1832 Ecuador annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a>, and the constitution of 1835 adopted the title of republic. The new state was poor, regionally divided and burdened with a share of Gran Colombia’s foreign debt.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guayaquil.jpg" alt="Guayaquil" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faro_de_Guayaquil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Padaguan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ecuador’s history has been shaped by three regions: the densely settled Andean highlands (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-ecuador">the Sierra</a>), the tropical Pacific coast (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-ecuador">Costa</a>) and the Amazonian lowlands (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriente-ecuador">Oriente</a>). In the Sierra, great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> bound indigenous labourers through debt servitude known as <em>concertaje</em> and through the <em>huasipungo</em> system, in which families worked the estate in return for a small plot of their own; the special tribute levied on indigenous people was abolished only in 1857, and the huasipungo only by the agrarian reform of 1964. On the coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> grown on large plantations, and between about 1880 and 1920 Ecuador was among the world’s leading cacao producers until plant disease and falling prices ruined the trade in the 1920s. After 1948 bananas transformed the coast, and by the early 1950s Ecuador had become the world’s largest banana exporter, a position it has held for most of the time since. Oil from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, flowing through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-ecuadorian-oil-pipeline-system">Trans-Ecuadorian pipeline</a> from 1972, then became the main source of export and state revenue, funding roads, schools and a larger middle class but also bringing debt and pollution. The Catholic Church long dominated education and social life. Today Ecuador has about 18 million people, mostly mestizo, with large indigenous, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afro-ecuadorians">Afro-Ecuadorian</a> and montubio minorities, and remittances from emigrants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> are an important source of income.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zarumilla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-zarumilla.jpg" alt="Battle of Zarumilla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zarumilla">Battle of Zarumilla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecuador_en_guerra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SuperMundo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of its history Ecuador’s armed forces mattered more in domestic politics than in foreign war. Early presidents depended on veterans of the independence wars, many of them Venezuelan or Colombian officers, and civil wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> factions recurred throughout the nineteenth century. In the crisis of 1859–1860, when the country split among rival governments and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> blockaded Guayaquil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-garcia-moreno">Gabriel García Moreno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flores-guatemala">Flores</a> retook the port in September 1860 and restored a single government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eloy-alfaro">Eloy Alfaro</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1895">Liberal Revolution of 1895</a> was likewise won in campaigns from the coast into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-ecuador">the Sierra</a>. The great external threat was Peru, which disputed the Amazonian territories that Ecuador claimed as heir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/real-audiencia-of-quito">Audiencia of Quito</a>. In July 1941 Peruvian forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-oro-province">El Oro</a> province and defeated the much smaller Ecuadorian army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zarumilla">Battle of Zarumilla</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-protocol">Rio de Janeiro Protocol</a> of January 1942, guaranteed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, Chile and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, assigned most of the disputed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> to Peru. Ecuador later declared the protocol null, and fighting flared again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paquisha-war">Paquisha War</a> of 1981 and the Cenepa War of 1995, in which Ecuadorian forces held their positions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia-presidential-act">Brasília Presidential Act</a> of October 1998 finally fixed the border. The military ruled directly in 1963–1966 and 1972–1979, and in recent years it has been deployed against organised crime.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-garcia-moreno"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gabriel-garcia-moreno.jpg" alt="Gabriel García Moreno" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-garcia-moreno">Gabriel García Moreno</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gabriel_Garc%C3%ADa_Moreno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Presidencia de la República del Ecuador, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ecuador has been a presidential republic throughout, but one of unusual constitutional instability: it has adopted some twenty constitutions since 1830, the latest in 2008. In the nineteenth century politics turned on the rivalry between conservative, clerical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and liberal, commercial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-garcia-moreno">Gabriel García Moreno</a>, president in 1861–1865 and again from 1869, built the most coherent state of the century on Catholic foundations: a concordat with the Holy See gave the church control of education, the constitution of 1869 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> a condition of citizenship, and in 1873 he consecrated the nation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-heart">Sacred Heart of Jesus</a>. He also built roads and schools and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/escuela-politecnica-nacional">Escuela Politécnica Nacional</a> before his assassination in Quito on 6 August 1875. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eloy-alfaro">Eloy Alfaro</a>, president in 1895–1901 and 1906–1911, and his Liberal allies reversed this programme with civil marriage and divorce (1902), secular public education and the constitution of 1906, which separated church and state. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-revolution">the Julian Revolution</a> of 1925, a reformist government advised by the American economist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-kemmerer">Edwin Kemmerer</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-bank-of-ecuador">Central Bank of Ecuador</a> in 1927. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-velasco-ibarra">José María Velasco Ibarra</a> held the presidency five times between 1934 and 1972 but completed only one full term, and was overthrown four times. Since 1979 Ecuador has been a democracy, though three elected presidents were removed before the end of their terms between 1997 and 2005.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hms-beagle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hms-beagle.jpg" alt="HMS Beagle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hms-beagle">HMS Beagle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PSM_V57_D097_Hms_beagle_in_the_straits_of_magellan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">R. T. Pritchett, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ecuador’s influence has been regional rather than global, but several of its contributions reached far. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a>, visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a> aboard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-beagle">HMS Beagle</a> in 1835, supplied observations that later shaped his theory of evolution by natural selection, and in 1978 the islands and the historic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> were among the first sites inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO’s World Heritage List</a>. Ecuadorian writers and artists, from the essayist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-montalvo">Juan Montalvo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-garcia-moreno">García Moreno</a>’s fiercest liberal critic, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-icaza">Jorge Icaza</a>, whose novel <em>Huasipungo</em> (1934) exposed the exploitation of highland indigenous labourers, and the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswaldo-guayasamin">Oswaldo Guayasamín</a>, gave the country a distinctive voice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American culture. From 1990 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conaie">Confederation of Indigenous Nationalities of Ecuador</a> (CONAIE), founded in 1986, became one of the most powerful indigenous movements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>: its uprising of June 1990 forced indigenous land and cultural rights onto the national agenda, and its mobilisations helped to topple presidents in 1997 and 2000. The constitution of 2008 was the first in the world to grant enforceable rights to nature, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachamama">Pachamama</a>, and declared Ecuador a plurinational state, ideas that have since been widely studied and imitated by environmental lawyers. Ecuador’s adoption of the US dollar in 2000 also made it a leading example of full dollarisation by a sovereign state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jaime-roldos-aguilera"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaime-roldos-aguilera.jpg" alt="Jaime Roldós Aguilera" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jaime-roldos-aguilera">Jaime Roldós Aguilera</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaime_Rold%C3%B3s_Aguilera_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Juan Serrano, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ecuador still exists, and its present form took shape after the military handed power back to civilians. A referendum approved a new constitution in 1978, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaime-roldos-aguilera">Jaime Roldós</a> took office in 1979 as the first president of the democratic era, dying in an air crash in 1981. The oil-financed boom gave way to the debt crisis of the 1980s, and in 1998–1999 falling oil prices, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> floods and a banking collapse led the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamil-mahuad">Jamil Mahuad</a> to freeze bank deposits. In January 2000 Mahuad announced that the national currency, the sucre, would be replaced by the US dollar; days later indigenous protesters and junior officers forced him from office, but dollarisation went ahead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-correa">Rafael Correa</a>, president from 2007 to 2017, used high oil revenues for public investment, defaulted on part of the foreign debt in 2008 and promoted the Montecristi constitution of 2008, while concentrating power and clashing with the press and indigenous organisations. His successors faced debt and mass protests, and in 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guillermo-lasso">Guillermo Lasso</a> dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> under the constitutional clause known as <em>muerte cruzada</em>. Ecuador, once relatively peaceful, has since become a major transit route for cocaine; after the murder of the presidential candidate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-villavicencio">Fernando Villavicencio</a> in August 2023, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-noboa">Daniel Noboa</a> declared an internal armed conflict against criminal gangs in January 2024.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ecuador shows how a small Andean republic, born from the break-up of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> Colombia and long divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>, was remade by successive export booms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, bananas and oil, and became a testing ground for indigenous politics, dollarisation and the legal rights of nature.</em></p>

